<h2 id="who-we-are-and-what-this-policy-covers">Who we are and what this policy covers</h2>
<p>Catbird is an independent app for Bluesky and the AT Protocol network. This policy covers the release version of Catbird for iPhone and iPad, Catbird’s supporting services and catbird.blue. “Catbird,” “we” and “us” mean <strong>Josh LaCalamito, United States</strong>. For privacy requests, support or abuse involving Catbird’s services, contact <strong><a href="mailto:hi@catbird.blue">hi@catbird.blue</a></strong>.</p>
<p>Your account provider, also called a Personal Data Server or PDS, hosts your account. Bluesky and other network services handle feeds, ordinary Bluesky messages and other network functions under their own policies. For Bluesky-hosted services, see <a href="https://bsky.social/about/support/privacy-policy">Bluesky’s Privacy Policy</a>. This policy does not govern every independent service or person who receives public network content.</p>
<h2 id="sign-in-and-account-requests">Sign in and account requests</h2>
<p>Nest is Catbird’s server. In the normal OAuth sign-in flow, Catbird opens your account provider’s authorization page. You enter your password there and authorize access; your password goes to that provider. Nest receives the upstream OAuth session credentials. Your device keeps a separate credential for its Nest session.</p>
<p>Most authenticated actions pass through Nest to the relevant account or network service. Nest can read the requests and responses it forwards, including posts, uploads and ordinary Bluesky direct messages. This processing does not mean that every request or message is saved in a Catbird database. Your account and network content remain hosted by the relevant upstream services, while Catbird keeps the records described below.</p>
<p><strong>Ordinary Bluesky direct messages are not end-to-end encrypted.</strong> Nest can read messages it relays. This version of Catbird does not include MLS encrypted chat or Circles.</p>
<p>Some requests go directly from your device to other services, including media and image servers, identity discovery, public account lookups, link-card extraction and video upload. Direct video upload can use a short-lived service authorization token on your device. GIF searches use Catbird’s forwarding service. When you load an embedded player or follow a link, the destination service receives that request. A direct recipient can receive your IP address and the information sent with the request.</p>
<h2 id="information-used-to-provide-catbird">Information used to provide Catbird</h2>
